## Per-AC-A reasoning

**AC-A1 (PRD AC-4) — network-unreachable classification.** Genuinely
testable pure logic. Covered by `test/request-failure.test.ts`'s
"network-unreachable failures" describe block: a raw `fetch()`-style
`TypeError` with no `status` field, a generic `Error` with no `status`
field (guards against an implementation that keys off `instanceof
TypeError` instead of the documented structural `status` check), and a
bare `{}` object with no `status` field at all. Verdict: **NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.

**AC-A2 (PRD AC-4) — HTTP-level classification, distinct from
network-unreachable.** Also genuinely testable pure logic. Covered by the
"HTTP-level failures" describe block: status 401, status 500 (both
individually asserted to produce a message naming that status — not just a
`kind` check, since a message that doesn't actually name the status would
leave the owner unable to tell one HTTP error from another), a
cross-status distinctness assertion (catches a hard-coded message bug), an
ApiError-shaped plain object with extra fields (404 + message + name, still
duck-typed correctly), and a cross-kind distinctness assertion (the
"server-unreachable" message must differ from any "http-error" message —
directly serves AC-A2's "visibly distinct" requirement). Verdict:
**NEW_TEST_REQUIRED**.

**Boundary cases (both AC-A1 and AC-A2 — malformed/edge causes).** Added
beyond the plan's two named cases because the plan's own Task 1 prose
specifies the check as "an object carrying a **numeric** `status` field" —
that adjective is a discriminative contract, not decoration, and a very
plausible bug is checking `"status" in cause` without `typeof cause.status
=== "number"`. Covered: a cause with `status` as a *string* ("500") must
NOT classify as http-error; a non-object primitive cause (a bare string)
must classify as server-unreachable without throwing; `null` and
`undefined` causes must classify as server-unreachable without throwing (a
naive `cause.status` property access on either would crash instead of
classifying — this is exactly the case that catches that class of bug,
raised explicitly in this session's dispatch instructions). These are
folded into the AC-A1/AC-A2 outcome rows below rather than a separate PRD
AC, since they exercise the same classifier contract from the malformed-input
side.

**AC-A3 (PRD AC-4) — 401 short-circuit in `handleFailure` stays untouched.**
This is a React/DOM code-path assertion (`cause instanceof ApiError &&
cause.status === 401` inside `src/app/App.tsx`'s `handleFailure`), not a
call into the pure classifier — Task 2 keeps this branch running *before*
`classifyRequestFailure` is ever reached, and it depends on real `ApiError`
`instanceof` behavior, `onUnauthorized()` being called, and React state
transitions, none of which workerd/Vitest can observe without a DOM/React
renderer that this project does not have (`docs/context/testing.md`; no
jsdom, ADR-0005 exact pins forbid adding one). Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED**
— covered by the plan's own Level 2 content-invariant grep check (asserts
`classifyRequestFailure` is wired into `App.tsx`) and Level 3 MANUAL step 3
(confirms the 401 case still returns to the token screen).

**AC-A4 (PRD AC-4) — typed text preserved, locked in via comment.** Task 3
is a doc-comment-only edit above the pre-existing `setTitle("")` sequencing
in `App.tsx`'s submit handler — "no functional change" per the plan's own
`**ACTION**` prose. What is asserted is a comment substring's presence in
source text, and the actual observable behavior (the input's DOM value
surviving a failed save) is React-rendered and unreachable from workerd.
Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED** — covered by the plan's own Level 2 grep
check (`"typed text is not lost"`) and Level 3 MANUAL step 1.

**AC-A5 (PRD AC-4) — uniform application to `refresh()` failures, not only
saves.** `classifyRequestFailure`'s signature (`(cause: unknown) => {...}`)
takes no caller-context parameter at all, so uniformity across every
`handleFailure` call site is structurally guaranteed by the classifier's own
shape and is implicitly exercised by every case in
`test/request-failure.test.ts` — there is no separate "called from
`refresh()`" branch inside the classifier to test differently. What AC-A5
actually asserts beyond that — that `handleFailure` really is the single
sink both `run()` and `refresh()` route through, and that the `{tasks ===
null && error === null && ...}` Loading guard does not fire once `error` is
set — is `App.tsx` control flow and JSX conditional rendering, DOM/React and
unreachable from workerd. Verdict: **NO_TEST_REQUIRED** for the wiring
half; the classification half is already covered by AC-A1/AC-A2's tests.

